	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}
$$

Suppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  

$$
\{\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\ {q}_{2}=\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\ {q}_{3}=\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\end{aligned}
$$

How do we find $R?$
First, let's rewrite the vectors $\mathbf{a}_1, \mathbf{a}_2, \mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\mathbf{q}_1, \mathbf{q}_2, \mathbf{q}_3 \mathbin{:}$
$$
{\left\{  \begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{q}}_{1} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{2}} {\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{2} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}} {\mathbf{a}}_{2} + 3 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{3} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{4}} {\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 2 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\mathbf{q}}_{2}\end{array} \right. }
